<header><p class="st-kicker">other · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Government of Oregon</h1><p class="st-meta">1843 CE – 1849 CE · capital Oregon City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-made government formed by American and Canadian settlers in the Willamette Valley while Britain and the United States both claimed Oregon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under an 1818 agreement, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> jointly occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-country">Oregon Country</a>, but neither governed it. Settlers, missionaries and retired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> employees in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willamette-valley">Willamette Valley</a> needed law for property and estates. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champoeg">Champoeg</a> on 2 May 1843 they voted narrowly to create a provisional government, and wagon trains on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-trail">Oregon Trail</a> soon swelled their numbers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organic-laws-of-oregon">Organic Laws</a> provided for a legislature, courts and land claims of up to 640 acres. A three-man executive committee was replaced in 1845 by a single governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-abernethy">George Abernethy</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-city-oregon">Oregon City</a>. It levied voluntary taxes, banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> but also passed laws excluding black settlers, and raised militia for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayuse-war">Cayuse War</a> after the Whitman killings of 1847.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-treaty">Oregon Treaty</a> of 1846 gave the area south of the 49th parallel to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. Congress created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-territory">Oregon Territory</a> in 1848, and the provisional government ended when Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-lane">Joseph Lane</a> arrived in March 1849.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/provisional-government-of-oregon.jpg" alt="Provisional Government of Oregon" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: UnknownThe original uploader was Aboutmovies at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oregon_provisional_districts_1843.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1843 CE – 1849 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oregon City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>George Abernethy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oregon Provisional Government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=provisional-government-of-oregon" aria-label="Provisional Government of Oregon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1769 577 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Provisional Government of Oregon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2089 833L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2089 833h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2089 833h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Oregon City. <a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=provisional-government-of-oregon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
